## The brief to hand blog-forge

```
Write a new blog post using the blog-authoring skill (vendored at
.claude/blog-forge — read its SKILL.md and references directly).

TOPIC
The foundations of image processing, taught as signal processing.

WHAT THIS POST IS
A pillar/parent post. Its job is to tell the reader what image processing
actually is and everything it considers, from first principles, through the
math and the signal view. It is the root of a series: every area it names gets
its own dedicated child post later. So it establishes the frame and the shared
math, and it stops there.

WHAT THIS POST IS NOT
Not a survey of every image-processing algorithm. Not a tutorial for any single
algorithm. Where an area deserves depth, state the one idea that belongs in the
frame and explicitly mark it as a future child post rather than expanding it here.

TIER AND AUDIENCE
Tier L4 (pillar): 7,500–10,000 words, 12–18 figures, 25–40 sources.
difficulty: advanced. category: algorithms.
Reader: a CS student or working engineer who can code but freezes at the math.

THE THREE THINGS TO STAY STRICTLY ON
1. The math — the actual operators, not their names.
2. The signal view — an image as a 2-D signal, and what each operation does to it.
3. OpenCV, advanced level — used as the lens for what the math looks like in
   practice, including the places where the API and the math disagree.

THE HARD REQUIREMENT ON HOW IT TEACHES
Every piece of math must be paired with a very simple, concrete explanation of
how it is actually evaluated. Readers do not fail on the symbols, they fail on
not knowing what is being computed, what "detecting the signal" means as an
operation, and what an experiment on this would even look like. So for each
operator: show it on small real numbers by hand first, then the general form,
then one plain sentence saying what it means. Never present a symbol before a
number.

EXPERIMENTS AND CODE — READ THIS CAREFULLY
Do not install any packages. Do not create or run a notebook. Nothing gets
persisted to the environment. All math is worked by hand in the post.
Consequently: no measured charts, no timings, no benchmark numbers. If a curve
is shown it is labelled in the caption as a model, never as measurement.
Code in the post is illustrative and explicitly marked as not executed here.
Close with a section that shows the tooling concretely: which libraries
(OpenCV, NumPy, and what else) and which specific functions map to which piece
of the math the post just taught — so the reader knows exactly what to reach
for when they do run it themselves.

WORKED EXAMPLE
One small grayscale patch, traced fully by hand with real integer values, small
enough to follow on paper. Reuse those same numbers later when the general form
appears, so the abstraction collapses back onto something the reader already
computed.

FIGURES
All figures are diagrams and math illustrations — no data plots, since there is
no measured data. Every figure must be our own redraw, referenced by the prose,
numbered, captioned, and given alt text.

RESEARCH
Run the full L4 research pass before drafting. Primary sources: the standard
image-processing textbook literature, the original papers for any named
operator, the OpenCV official documentation, and OpenCV source where the
documented behaviour and the actual behaviour differ. Do not skip the
disagreement rung — contested attributions, operators whose optimality claims
depend on assumptions readers do not check, and API behaviour that does not
match its name are exactly the material this post needs.

FRONTMATTER
Carry both schemas in one block: the blog-forge fields the validator requires
(title, slug, date, summary, tags, category, tier, difficulty,
reading_minutes) plus the keys the canery parser reads (categories,
cover_image_url, cover_image_alt). Extra keys are ignored by the platform, so
one block satisfies both.

PIPELINE
Run phase 0 through the checkpoints. Stop at Checkpoint A with the source list,
and at Checkpoint B with the outline and figure plan — do not draft prose before
the outline is approved. Title and slug are settled at Checkpoint B.
```

